  "text": "The offending material included selections from Plato’s Symposium, one of his most important and widely read works. In it, the philosopher Socrates and his companions at a banquet discuss the nature of love. And in one passage, the playwright Aristophanes offers an account of how love and sexuality came to be. There were once three sexes, he says – male, female and androgynous – and all humans were, at first, physically joined pairs. But after we mounted an attack on the gods, Zeus split us in two, and we’ve ached to complete ourselves with partners – of the same or another sex – ever since. The “gender ideology” of this tale comes to us from the fourth century BC.",
